### How does Kafka balance load across consumers?

Load balancing in Kafka means fair distribution of partitions. The group coordinator and the elected group leader compute an assignment using the configured `partition.assignment.strategy`. The goal is that each consumer owns about the same number of partitions so the processing load is even.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.Properties;

public class BalancedConsumerConfig {

    public static Properties createConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-group");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");

        // Cooperative sticky for minimal rebalance disruption
        props.put("partition.assignment.strategy",
            "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor");

        // Heartbeat and session timeout tuning
        props.put("heartbeat.interval.ms", "3000");
        props.put("session.timeout.ms", "10000");
        props.put("max.poll.interval.ms", "300000");

        System.out.println("Consumer configured for balanced load distribution");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        createConfig();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Consumer configured for balanced load distribution

With 12 partitions and 4 consumers:
  Consumer A: P0, P1, P2  (3 partitions)
  Consumer B: P3, P4, P5  (3 partitions)
  Consumer C: P6, P7, P8  (3 partitions)
  Consumer D: P9, P10, P11 (3 partitions)
```

### What are the partition assignment strategies?

Kafka offers four built-in strategies. Each strategy divides partitions differently, and the choice affects how stable assignments are during rebalances.

| Strategy | How It Works | Stability | Recommended |
|---|---|---|---|
| RangeAssignor | Contiguous ranges per topic | Low | No |
| RoundRobinAssignor | Spread one by one evenly | Medium | Development |
| StickyAssignor | Minimize movement on rebalance | High | Yes |
| CooperativeStickyAssignor | Sticky + incremental rebalance | Highest | Production |

```java
package com.bank.assignment;

public class AssignmentStrategyComparison {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("RangeAssignor (3 consumers, 6 partitions):");
        System.out.println("  A: P0, P1  |  B: P2, P3  |  C: P4, P5");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("RoundRobinAssignor:");
        System.out.println("  A: P0, P3  |  B: P1, P4  |  C: P2, P5");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CooperativeStickyAssignor (D joins):");
        System.out.println("  Before: A:P0,P1 B:P2,P3 C:P4,P5");
        System.out.println("  After:  A:P0 B:P2,P3 C:P4,P5 D:P1");
        System.out.println("  Only P1 moved (minimal disruption)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
RangeAssignor (3 consumers, 6 partitions):
  A: P0, P1  |  B: P2, P3  |  C: P4, P5

RoundRobinAssignor:
  A: P0, P3  |  B: P1, P4  |  C: P2, P5

CooperativeStickyAssignor (D joins):
  Before: A:P0,P1 B:P2,P3 C:P4,P5
  After:  A:P0 B:P2,P3 C:P4,P5 D:P1
  Only P1 moved (minimal disruption)
```

### What triggers a rebalance?

Three events trigger a rebalance. A consumer joining the group (scaling up), a consumer leaving gracefully (clean shutdown), and a consumer failing (crash or stuck processing that causes missed heartbeats).

```java
package com.bank.rebalance;

public class RebalanceTriggers {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rebalance Triggers:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("1. Consumer JOINS (scaling up):");
        System.out.println("   New instance starts -> coordinator redistributes");
        System.out.println("   Some partitions move from existing to new consumer");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("2. Consumer LEAVES (graceful shutdown):");
        System.out.println("   Instance sends LeaveGroup -> immediate reassignment");
        System.out.println("   Fast because coordinator knows immediately");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3. Consumer FAILS (crash or stuck):");
        System.out.println("   Heartbeats stop -> coordinator waits session.timeout.ms");
        System.out.println("   After timeout -> declares dead -> reassigns partitions");
        System.out.println("   Slower because of timeout wait");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Rebalance Triggers:

1. Consumer JOINS: new instance -> coordinator redistributes
2. Consumer LEAVES: sends LeaveGroup -> immediate reassignment
3. Consumer FAILS: heartbeats stop -> wait session.timeout.ms -> reassign
```

### How does heartbeat and session timeout work?

Each consumer sends a heartbeat on a background thread every `heartbeat.interval.ms`. If the coordinator does not hear from a consumer within `session.timeout.ms`, it declares that consumer dead and starts a rebalance. There is also `max.poll.interval.ms`: if a consumer takes too long between calls to `poll()`, the coordinator removes it.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.Properties;

public class HeartbeatConfig {

    public static Properties configureTimeouts() {
        Properties props = new Properties();

        // Heartbeat sent every 3 seconds
        props.put("heartbeat.interval.ms", "3000");

        // If no heartbeat for 10 seconds, consumer is dead
        props.put("session.timeout.ms", "10000");

        // If no poll() call for 5 minutes, consumer is stuck
        props.put("max.poll.interval.ms", "300000");

        System.out.println("Heartbeat: every 3s");
        System.out.println("Session timeout: 10s (dead if no heartbeat)");
        System.out.println("Max poll interval: 300s (stuck if no poll)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Rule: heartbeat.interval.ms < session.timeout.ms / 3");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        configureTimeouts();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Heartbeat: every 3s
Session timeout: 10s (dead if no heartbeat)
Max poll interval: 300s (stuck if no poll)

Rule: heartbeat.interval.ms < session.timeout.ms / 3
```

### How does eager vs cooperative rebalancing differ?

In the classic eager protocol, every consumer revokes all its partitions, the leader computes a fresh assignment, and consumers receive new partitions. This causes a stop-the-world pause. The cooperative incremental protocol only revokes the specific partitions that need to move, keeping the rest flowing.

```java
package com.bank.rebalance;

public class EagerVsCooperativeRebalance {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Eager Rebalance (stop-the-world):");
        System.out.println("  Step 1: ALL consumers stop processing");
        System.out.println("  Step 2: ALL consumers revoke ALL partitions");
        System.out.println("  Step 3: Leader computes new assignment");
        System.out.println("  Step 4: ALL consumers receive new assignment");
        System.out.println("  Step 5: Processing resumes");
        System.out.println("  Impact: FULL PAUSE for entire group");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cooperative Rebalance (incremental):");
        System.out.println("  Step 1: Only affected consumers revoke partitions");
        System.out.println("  Step 2: Other consumers KEEP processing");
        System.out.println("  Step 3: Revoked partitions assigned to new consumer");
        System.out.println("  Impact: MINIMAL PAUSE, most partitions unaffected");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Eager Rebalance: FULL PAUSE for entire group
Cooperative Rebalance: MINIMAL PAUSE, most partitions unaffected
```

### What is static group membership?

Static group membership via `group.instance.id` avoids rebalances during brief restarts. When a consumer with a static instance id restarts within `session.timeout.ms`, it reclaims its previous partitions without triggering a rebalance. This is essential for rolling deployments.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.Properties;

public class StaticMembershipConfig {

    public static Properties createConfig(String instanceId) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-group");

        // Static membership: no rebalance on brief restart
        props.put("group.instance.id", instanceId);
        props.put("session.timeout.ms", "30000");

        System.out.println("Static member: " + instanceId);
        System.out.println("If restarted within 30s, reclaims partitions");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        createConfig("settlement-instance-1");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Static member: settlement-instance-1
If restarted within 30s, reclaims partitions

Without static membership:
  Restart -> LeaveGroup -> rebalance -> JoinGroup -> rebalance again
  Two rebalances per restart!

With static membership:
  Restart -> rejoin with same instance id -> reclaim partitions
  Zero rebalances if restart < session.timeout.ms
```

### What are common mistakes with rebalancing?

The most common mistake is not tuning `max.poll.interval.ms` for slow consumers. If processing takes longer than this interval, the coordinator removes the consumer, triggering an unnecessary rebalance and causing duplicate processing.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// WRONG: Default max.poll.interval.ms (5 minutes) with slow processing.
// If a batch takes 6 minutes, the consumer is evicted and rebalanced.
public class WrongPollInterval {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: max.poll.interval.ms = 300000 (5 min)");
        System.out.println("Processing a batch of transactions takes 6 min");
        System.out.println("Result: consumer evicted, rebalance triggered");
        System.out.println("Partitions reassigned, messages reprocessed");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// CORRECT: Set max.poll.interval.ms > max expected processing time.
// Or reduce max.poll.records to process smaller batches faster.
public class CorrectPollInterval {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CORRECT: max.poll.interval.ms = 600000 (10 min)");
        System.out.println("Or: max.poll.records = 50 (smaller batches)");
        System.out.println("Result: consumer stays alive, no false eviction");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: slow processing exceeds max.poll.interval.ms -> false eviction
CORRECT: increase interval or reduce batch size -> stable group
```

### What should a senior engineer know about rebalancing?

Rebalances are necessary but costly, so you minimize unnecessary ones. Use the cooperative sticky assignor, tune poll intervals, use static membership for rolling restarts, and monitor rebalance frequency. Frequent rebalances directly cause consumer lag and duplicate processing.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class RebalanceBestPractices {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior Rebalancing Best Practices:");
        System.out.println("1. Use CooperativeStickyAssignor (no stop-the-world)");
        System.out.println("2. Set max.poll.interval.ms > max processing time");
        System.out.println("3. Use static group membership for rolling deploys");
        System.out.println("4. Set max.poll.records to control batch size");
        System.out.println("5. Monitor rebalance frequency (high = problem)");
        System.out.println("6. Commit offsets in onPartitionsRevoked callback");
        System.out.println("7. Investigate root cause of frequent rebalances");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Senior Rebalancing Best Practices:
1. Use CooperativeStickyAssignor
2. Set max.poll.interval.ms > max processing time
3. Use static group membership for rolling deploys
4. Set max.poll.records to control batch size
5. Monitor rebalance frequency
6. Commit offsets in onPartitionsRevoked callback
7. Investigate root cause of frequent rebalances
```
